# Give Lines Custom Names

You can name lines when defining the grid, then reference them in placement:
```css
.container {
  grid-template-columns: [sidebar-start] 200px [sidebar-end main-start] 1fr [main-end];
}
.item {
  grid-column: main-start / main-end;
}
```
Names are enclosed in square brackets. A line can have multiple names. This makes layout more semantic.

# All Four Lines at Once

`grid-area` can accept four line values in the order: `row-start / column-start / row-end / column-end` (a bit counter‑intuitive).

```css
.item {
  grid-area: 1 / 1 / 3 / 3;
  /* row start 1, column start 1, row end 3, column end 3 */
}
```
This places the item spanning the first two rows and first two columns. The same as `grid-row: 1 / 3; grid-column: 1 / 3;`.

We'll see a different use of `grid-area` with template areas later.

slides.codeSnippet:
.container {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
  grid-template-rows: 1fr 1fr;
}
.item1 { grid-area: 1 / 1 / 3 / 2; } /* spans two rows, one column */
.item2 { grid-area: 1 / 2 / 2 / 3; }
.item3 { grid-area: 2 / 2 / 3 / 3; }
/* item1 overlaps the row where items 2 and 3 sit, but it's in col 1 */
